WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.536
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online B one, Book one.

2
00:00:05.836 --> 00:00:09.083
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.383 --> 00:00:15.472
Unit three: My Family Tree. Today you will describe your relatives and family relationships.

4
00:00:15.772 --> 00:00:18.668
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:23.052
Part one: words. You will learn twelve new words.

6
00:00:23.917 --> 00:00:24.999
Relative.

7
00:00:25.983 --> 00:00:28.296
Relative means a person in your family.

8
00:00:28.883 --> 00:00:30.674
Many relatives came to the wedding.

9
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:33.843
Your turn. Say: Relative.

10
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:37.724
Relative.

11
00:00:38.483 --> 00:00:39.217
Sibling.

12
00:00:40.217 --> 00:00:42.588
Sibling means a brother or a sister.

13
00:00:43.183 --> 00:00:44.556
I have two siblings.

14
00:00:45.150 --> 00:00:47.419
Your turn. Say: Sibling.

15
00:00:50.267 --> 00:00:51.117
Sibling.

16
00:00:51.883 --> 00:00:52.942
Ancestor.

17
00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:56.960
Ancestor means a family member who lived long ago.

18
00:00:57.550 --> 00:01:00.211
We pray for our ancestors at Pchum Ben.

19
00:01:00.817 --> 00:01:03.365
Your turn. Say: Ancestor.

20
00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.306
Ancestor.

21
00:01:08.050 --> 00:01:09.109
Generation.

22
00:01:10.117 --> 00:01:13.440
Generation means all the people born at about the same time.

23
00:01:14.050 --> 00:01:16.084
Three generations live in our house.

24
00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:19.057
Your turn. Say: Generation.

25
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:23.006
Generation.

26
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:24.786
Household.

27
00:01:25.783 --> 00:01:28.329
Household means all the people who live in one home.

28
00:01:28.917 --> 00:01:30.928
Our household has seven people.

29
00:01:31.517 --> 00:01:34.006
Your turn. Say: Household.

30
00:01:36.867 --> 00:01:37.810
Household.

31
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:39.377
Close-knit.

32
00:01:40.383 --> 00:01:43.428
Close-knit means very close and supportive of each other.

33
00:01:44.017 --> 00:01:45.865
We are a close-knit family.

34
00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:49.590
Your turn. Say: Close-knit.

35
00:01:52.433 --> 00:01:53.492
Close-knit.

36
00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:55.576
Extended family.

37
00:01:56.583 --> 00:02:01.311
Extended family means family beyond parents and children: aunts, uncles, cousins.

38
00:02:01.917 --> 00:02:04.288
My extended family meets at New Year.

39
00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:07.919
Your turn. Say: Extended family.

40
00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.093
Extended family.

41
00:02:12.850 --> 00:02:13.979
Only child.

42
00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:18.457
Only child means a child with no brothers or sisters.

43
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:21.003
My cousin is an only child.

44
00:02:21.617 --> 00:02:24.432
Your turn. Say: Only child.

45
00:02:27.267 --> 00:02:28.593
Only child.

46
00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:30.084
Twin.

47
00:02:31.083 --> 00:02:34.151
Twin means one of two children born at the same time.

48
00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.529
My twin and I look the same.

49
00:02:37.117 --> 00:02:39.339
Your turn. Say: Twin.

50
00:02:42.200 --> 00:02:42.841
Twin.

51
00:02:43.583 --> 00:02:44.422
Resemble.

52
00:02:45.417 --> 00:02:47.405
Resemble means to look like someone.

53
00:02:48.017 --> 00:02:49.691
I resemble my grandmother.

54
00:02:50.283 --> 00:02:52.576
Your turn. Say: Resemble.

55
00:02:55.433 --> 00:02:56.469
Resemble.

56
00:02:57.217 --> 00:02:57.997
Raise.

57
00:02:58.983 --> 00:03:02.005
Raise means to look after a child until they are an adult.

58
00:03:02.617 --> 00:03:04.709
My grandparents raised my father.

59
00:03:05.317 --> 00:03:07.853
Your turn. Say: Raise.

60
00:03:10.700 --> 00:03:11.678
Raise.

61
00:03:12.417 --> 00:03:13.197
Bond.

62
00:03:14.183 --> 00:03:16.879
Bond means a strong feeling that connects people.

63
00:03:17.483 --> 00:03:19.611
I have a strong bond with my sister.

64
00:03:20.217 --> 00:03:22.172
Your turn. Say: Bond.

65
00:03:25.033 --> 00:03:25.825
Bond.

66
00:03:26.633 --> 00:03:30.249
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

67
00:03:31.083 --> 00:03:33.269
Visal, is that photo of your family?

68
00:03:34.383 --> 00:03:38.382
Yes. Three generations live in our household.

69
00:03:39.483 --> 00:03:40.926
Is this bike yours?

70
00:03:42.017 --> 00:03:45.809
No, it isn't mine. It's my cousin's. Hers is red.

71
00:03:46.917 --> 00:03:48.684
Are you close to your relatives?

72
00:03:49.783 --> 00:03:54.560
Yes, we are close-knit. I have a strong bond with my grandmother.

73
00:03:55.700 --> 00:03:57.073
Part three: grammar.

74
00:03:57.917 --> 00:04:00.671
Grammar focus: Possessive pronouns.

75
00:04:00.971 --> 00:04:02.134
Look at the pattern.

76
00:04:02.850 --> 00:04:04.292
This book is mine.

77
00:04:05.183 --> 00:04:06.626
That bike is hers.

78
00:04:07.517 --> 00:04:09.052
That house is theirs.

79
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:15.694
Possessive pronouns replace 'my + noun': my bag → mine. Don't put a noun after them.

80
00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:19.718
Be careful! Don't say: This book is my.

81
00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:23.898
Say: This book is mine.

82
00:04:24.198 --> 00:04:27.672
At the end of a sentence, use mine, yours, hers or theirs.

83
00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.084
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

84
00:04:31.900 --> 00:04:33.458
Our Close-Knit Family.

85
00:04:33.758 --> 00:04:36.269
I come from a close-knit extended family.

86
00:04:37.267 --> 00:04:40.044
Three generations live together in our household.

87
00:04:41.033 --> 00:04:44.171
I have one sibling, and we both resemble our grandparents.

88
00:04:45.167 --> 00:04:48.165
My grandparents raised my father in this same house.

89
00:04:49.167 --> 00:04:51.886
The house is theirs, but they say it is ours too.

90
00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:56.979
Part five: quiz. Three questions. Are you ready?

91
00:04:57.817 --> 00:05:00.365
This pen is blank. (my pen)

92
00:05:00.665 --> 00:05:03.656
A, mine. B, my. C, me.

93
00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:08.828
The answer is A: mine.

94
00:05:09.983 --> 00:05:12.145
A brother or a sister is a blank.

95
00:05:12.445 --> 00:05:15.751
A, sibling. B, relative. C, twin.

96
00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:21.578
The answer is A: sibling.

97
00:05:22.717 --> 00:05:24.554
To look like someone is to blank them.

98
00:05:24.854 --> 00:05:28.054
A, resemble. B, raise. C, bond.

99
00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:34.052
The answer is A: resemble.

100
00:05:35.267 --> 00:05:38.476
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

101
00:05:39.317 --> 00:05:41.850
Describe one relative you are close to.

102
00:05:50.650 --> 00:05:52.081
Now listen to Visal.

103
00:05:52.381 --> 00:05:59.738
I am close to my grandmother. She raised me when my parents worked. I resemble her, and we have a strong bond.

104
00:06:00.038 --> 00:06:02.072
Excellent answer, Visal!

105
00:06:02.983 --> 00:06:07.447
Great work! Now you can use twelve new words and possessive pronouns.

106
00:06:07.747 --> 00:06:11.049
Today's value is family. Love and help your family.

107
00:06:11.349 --> 00:06:14.048
See you in Unit four. Goodbye!

108
00:06:14.348 --> 00:06:18.010
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

